"Suffridge's convolution theorem for polynomials and entire functions having only real zeros - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870815004387)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Date: 22 January 2016 ... # Suffridge's convolution theorem for polynomials and entire functions having only real zeros\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: 22 January 2016\n\nPages: 426-463\n\nVolume: Volume 288\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Suffridge's convolution theorem for polynomials and entire functions having only real zeros\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Martin Lamprecht ^{1}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2015.10.016\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe present a q-extension of Pólya's and Schur's characterization of multiplier sequences and a q-extension and a converse of Newton's inequalities.\n\n## MSC\n\n30C10\n\n;\n\n30C15\n\n;\n\n26C10\n\n;\n\n30D15\n\n;\n\n05A99\n\n## Keywords\n\nSuffridge polynomials\n\n;\n\nPólya–Schur multiplier sequences\n\n;\n\nq-Binomials\n\n;\n\nLog-concave sequences\n\n;\n\nNewton's inequalities\n\nThe research that led to this paper was conducted while I had a post-doc position at the department of mathematics at the University of Würzburg. I am very grateful to Stephan Ruscheweyh and all members of Lehrstuhl IV for the productive and friendly atmosphere there. I also would like to thank the referee for pointing out several interesting references and for inventing the term ‘q-function theory’.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSuffridge's Convolution Theorem for Polynomials with Zeros in the Unit Disk (https://eurekamag.com/research/108/221/108221049.php)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; In this paper, we show that Suffridge's convolution theorem holds for a certain class of polynomials with zeros in the unit disk and thus obtain an extension for one further special case of the Grace–Szegö convolution theorem. ... 2016: Suffridge's convolution theorem for polynomials and entire functions having only real zeros Advances in Mathematics 288: 426-463\n\n# Suffridge's Convolution Theorem for Polynomials with Zeros in the Unit Disk\n\n##### Lamprecht, M.\n\n#### Computational Methods and Function Theory 16(3): 433-455\n\n#### 2016\n\n* * *\n\nISSN/ISBN: 1617-9447\n\nDOI: 10.1007/s40315-015-0151-x\nAccession: 108221049\n\n##### Summary\n\nIn 1976, Suffridge proved an intriguing theorem regarding the convolution of polynomials with zeros only on the unit circle. His result generalizes a special case of the fundamental Grace–Szegö convolution theorem, but so far it is an open problem whether there is a Suffridge-like extension of the general Grace–Szegö convolution theorem. In this paper, we show that Suffridge's convolution theorem holds for a certain class of polynomials with zeros in the unit disk and thus obtain an extension for one further special case of the Grace–Szegö convolution theorem.\n\n#### References\n\nLamprecht, M. 2016: Suffridge's convolution theorem for polynomials and entire functions having only real zeros Advances in Mathematics 288: 426-463\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnalytic properties arising from the Baxter numbers (https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.05873)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Our approach is based on Dilks' framework of $(q,t)$-Hoggatt sums, which is a $q$-analog for Baxter permutations. Within this framework, we show that the family of $(1,t)$-Hoggatt sums satisfies the interlacing property using fundamental results on Hadamard products of polynomials.\nTitle: Analytic properties arising from the Baxter numbers\nAuthors: Hanqian Fang, Candice X. T. Zhang, James J. Y. Zhao\nDate: Fri May  9 08:24:02 2025\n\nBaxter numbers are known as the enumeration of Baxter permutations and numerous other discrete structures, playing a significant role across combinatorics, algebra, and analysis. In this paper, we focus on the analytic properties related to Baxter numbers. We prove that the descent polynomials of Baxter permutations have interlacing zeros, which is a property stronger than real-rootedness. Our approach is based on Dilks' framework of $(q,t)$-Hoggatt sums, which is a $q$-analog for Baxter permutations. Within this framework, we show that the family of $(1,t)$-Hoggatt sums satisfies the interlacing property using fundamental results on Hadamard products of polynomials. For Baxter numbers, we prove their asymptotic $r$-log-convexity via asymptotic expansions of $P$-recursive sequences. In particular, we confirm their $2$-log-convexity using symbolic computation techniques.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBIBLIOGRAPHY 112 (https://ul.qucosa.de/api/qucosa%3A82258/attachment/ATT-0/)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.0 years ago; “Suffridge’s convolution theorem for polynomials and entire functions having only real zeros”. In: *Advances in Mathematics* 288 (2016), pp. 426–463 (cit. on pp. ix, 3).\nBIBLIOGRAPHY 112\n\n[55] Edmond Laguerre. “Sur quelques points de la théorie des équations numériques”. In: *Acta mathematica* 4.1 (1884), pp. 97–120 (cit. on p. 6).\n\n[56] Martin Lamprecht. “Suffridge’s convolution theorem for polynomials and entire functions having only real zeros”. In: *Advances in Mathematics* 288 (2016), pp. 426–463 (cit. on pp. ix, 3).\n\n[57] Boris Yakovlevich Levin. *Distribution of zeros of entire functions*. Trans. by R. P. Boas et al. Revised edition. Translations of Mathematical Monographs 5. Providence, RI: American Mathematical Society, 1980 (cit. on pp. viii, 1–3, 5).\n\n[58] Morris Marden. *Geometry of polynomials*. 2nd ed. Mathematical Surveys 3. Providence, RI: American Mathematical Society, 1966 (cit. on pp. 8, 9).\n\n[59] Thu Hien Ngu